# DTF tier manifest: ingest-tenant-fairness  (#2598)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The gap is in the shared ingestion
# concurrency seam (backend/src/util/bounded_archive.rs), ahead of any storage
# backend, so filesystem is sufficient.
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS (#2598, follow-up to #2561): #2561 shipped a GLOBAL process-wide
# semaphore (MAX_CONCURRENT_INGEST_EXTRACTIONS, default 8) that fast-fails excess
# ingestion archive decodes with a 503. That cap is fair in aggregate but NOT per
# tenant: one noisy tenant (repository) firing many concurrent uploads can hold
# every global permit and starve other tenants, which then see 503s caused
# entirely by a neighbour. #2598 adds a per-tenant sub-limit on top of the global
# cap at the SAME acquire seam (fairness key = repository), so one tenant can
# never consume the whole global budget; the remainder always stays available to
# other tenants. `repo_visibility_middleware` attributes each format request to
# its repository, so every extractor inherits the fairness with no per-handler
# change.
#
# Deterministic caps for this tier: global=2, per-tenant=1. With per-tenant=1 a
# single repository holds at most ONE global permit at any instant, so of the two
# global permits at least one is ALWAYS free for a different repository —
# a deterministic GREEN for the cross-tenant probe. On a pre-#2598 image (no
# per-tenant layer) the noisy repository can hold BOTH global permits, so the
# neighbour's upload is shed with a 503 (RED).
#
# Discriminating oracle (RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL)   a normal chart upload to repo A and to repo B each succeed
#               (legit path unchanged).
#   (FAIRNESS)  while repo A is saturated with a sustained burst of concurrent
#               slow-to-decode chart uploads (each holds its decode permit while
#               a large-but-under-budget filler inflates), a normal upload to
#               repo B still SUCCEEDS (2xx) rather than 503.
#                 * GREEN on the fixed image: A is capped at its per-tenant
#                   sub-limit (1), leaving a global permit for B.
#                 * RED on a pre-#2598 image: A holds both global permits and B
#                   is starved (503).
#
# DETERMINISM CAVEAT: cross-tenant *starvation* is inherently timing-dependent
# over HTTP (a decode permit is held only for the duration of the synchronous
# decode). The GREEN direction this oracle asserts is deterministic — with
# per-tenant=1 a neighbour permit is structurally always available on the fix,
# independent of timing. The RED direction (that a pre-fix image starves B)
# depends on A actually holding both global permits at the instant B probes; the
# oracle drives that by keeping a sustained concurrent slow-upload burst against
# A for the whole probe window and probing B several times, but a pre-fix image
# under an unusually idle scheduler could still let a B probe slip through. The
# tier is therefore a reliable GREEN gate for the fix and a strong (not
# hermetic) RED signal for the regression.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login plus a burst of
# concurrent uploads; the base default (ON) would throttle them and mask the
# concurrency behaviour under test.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
export MAX_CONCURRENT_INGEST_EXTRACTIONS=2
export MAX_CONCURRENT_INGEST_EXTRACTIONS_PER_TENANT=1
